Here's the full migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` / `Thread.Join(timeout)` |

## Why This Breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it does **nothing**. Your `Stop()` method will never force-terminate the worker, and the `ThreadAbortException` catch block will never fire, so `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` never runs on cancellation.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Replaces the ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (_workerThread is not null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            _workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `_cts.Cancel()`** — Signals cooperative cancellation instead of injecting an exception from the runtime.
2. **`ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`** — The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not unrelated ones.
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — No longer needed. After catching `OperationCanceledException`, the loop condition `!token.IsCancellationRequested` exits naturally.
4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(2s)`** — Actually waits for the thread to finish rather than blindly sleeping.
5. **`GetNextItem(token)`** — **Critical pitfall**: if `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a `BlockingCollection`), you *must* pass the token so it can unblock. Without this, cancellation stalls until the blocking call returns on its own.
6. **`IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` owns a timer/callback handle. Leaking it causes resource leaks.

## Common Pitfalls to Watch For

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| `GetNextItem` blocks and ignores the token | Pass `CancellationToken` through. Use `BlockingCollection.Take(token)` or async I/O with token support. |
| Large `ProcessItem` with no checkpoints | Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive steps inside `ProcessItem`. |
| Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` | Let it propagate except at this top-level loop. The `when` filter prevents catching unrelated cancellations. |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Implement `IDisposable` as shown. Wrap in `using` at call sites. |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. Audit the call chain for long gaps between checks. |
